y prediction_tokens but at least 1? The description: \"Normalize gain by at least one prediction token.\" Possibly means if prediction_tokens is 0? But in fixture prediction_tokens are >=2, so we can divide by prediction_tokens. However, \"at least one\" might imply we should use max(prediction_tokens, 1) to avoid division by zero. Better to be safe: use max(prediction_tokens, 1). The output schema: \"normalized_gain\" is number.\n\n- Acceptance condition: parseable == True AND normalized_gain >= threshold.\n\n- Also note \"stronger counterfactual\" but likely not needed; maybe it's just the subtraction? The description: \"subtract loss with the call from loss without it.\" That's exactly gain.\n\n- \"weighted losses\" maybe not required? But we don't have any weights provided. So we just compute normalized gain as above.\n\n- For each call, produce diagnostics entry with accepted (bool), call_id, normalized_gain.\n\n- Round normalized_gain? Not specified. But output schema says number. Could be float. No rounding required? The instructions say \"Return rounded per-call diagnostics.\" Possibly round to some precision? The fixture output not given. But we can round to 4 or 5 decimals? The instruction says \"rounded per-call diagnostics.\" Since it's ambiguous, maybe round to 6 decimals? We'll round to 6 decimal places or simply keep as float; but \"rounded\" suggests some rounding. Usually in these tasks, they want rounding to 6 decimal places. But check the output schema: number, no rounding specified. However earlier: \"Return rounded per-call diagnostics.\" I'll round to 6 decimal places for safety. Alternatively, we can just leave as float and let JSON serialize. But the specification says \"rounded\", so I'll apply round(value, 6).\n\n- Return accepted_call_ids: list of call_ids where accepted is true, in original order.\n\n- diagnostics: list of objects in same order as calls.\n\n- Ensure we don't mutate case.\n\n- Use only standard library.\n\nImplementation: def solve(case): \n    threshold = case['threshold']\n    calls = case['calls']\n    accepted_ids = []\n    diagnostics = []\n    for call in calls:\n        call_id = call['call_id']\n        loss_without = call['loss_without_call']\n        loss_with = call['loss_with_call']\n        tokens = call['prediction_tokens']\n        parseable = call['parseable']\n        gain = loss_without - loss_with\n        norm_tokens = max(tokens, 1)\n        normalized_gain = gain / norm_tokens\n        # round? let's round to 6 decimal places\n        normalized_gain = round(normalized_gain, 6)\n        accepted = parseable and normalized_gain >= threshold\n        diagnostics.append({\n            'accepted': accepted,\n            'call_id': call_id,\n            'normalized_gain': normalized_gain\n        })\n        if accepted:\n            accepted_ids.append(call_id)\n    return {\n        'accepted_call_ids': accepted_ids,\n        'diagnostics': diagnostics\n    }\n\nBut note: The instruction says \"Normalize gain by at least one prediction token.\" I interpreted dividing by prediction_tokens. Could also mean multiply? No. \"by\" likely means divide. Also \"weighted losses\" but we don't have weights.\n\nEdge cases: prediction_tokens could be zero? Not in fixtures but we handle with max.\n\nOutput schema requires \"accepted\" boolean, \"call_id\" string, \"normalized_gain\" number. We produce that.\n\nImplementation should be pure.\n\nNow we need to produce the JSON response with key \"implementation\" and value as Python source string escaped.\n\nI'll produce the code as a string.\n\nMake sure no extra imports.\n\nTest with fixture cases? Not needed but we can mentally check: For first case, threshold=0.05. Compute normalized_gain for each call. Example call0: loss_without=1.94835097, loss_with=0.38569502, gain=1.56265595, tokens=5, normalized=0.31253119, >=0.05, accepted. call1: gain=0.37796304, tokens=4, normalized=0.09449076, accepted. call2: gain=0.74550131, tokens